# restore.conf.example - policy defaults for bin/restore.sh
#
# Copy this file to restore.conf (or anywhere else) and pass it with
# --config /path/to/restore.conf. Every value here can still be overridden
# by an explicit CLI flag - CLI flags always win over the config file,
# regardless of the order they appear on the command line.
#
# This file is parsed by a restricted KEY=VALUE reader, NOT sourced as
# bash - unknown keys are warned about and ignored, and there is no way
# for a config file to execute code. Blank lines and '#' comments are
# ignored. Do not quote values; everything after '=' up to end of line
# (minus a trailing comment) is taken literally.

# --home-strategy: one of skip | remap | preserve | prompt
#   skip     - do not set a home path; let TrueNAS assign the default.
#              This is the recommended default: pool/dataset layouts
#              rarely match exactly between source and target systems.
#   remap    - apply the HOME_REMAP rules below; falls back to skip for
#              any path that matches no rule.
#   preserve - use the backup's home path unchanged (warns if the
#              parent directory doesn't exist on the target).
#   prompt   - ask interactively for each user (not usable with --yes
#              or in a non-interactive shell; falls back to skip there).
HOME_STRATEGY=skip

# HOME_REMAP: one FROM=TO prefix rule per line; repeat the key for
# multiple rules. Only used when HOME_STRATEGY=remap. The first rule
# whose FROM is a prefix of the backup's home path wins.
#
# Example: a backup taken from a pool named "tank" being restored onto a
# new system where the equivalent pool is named "newpool":
#HOME_REMAP=/mnt/tank/home=/mnt/newpool/home
#HOME_REMAP=/mnt/tank/users=/mnt/newpool/users

# --shell-fallback: shell to substitute when a user's recorded shell does
# not exist on the target (checked live over SSH during restore).
SHELL_FALLBACK=/usr/bin/bash

# --yes-to-all-conflicts: skip | overwrite (leave unset to be prompted
# per-conflict, with the option to choose "apply to all" interactively
# partway through). Setting this here pre-seeds that "apply to all"
# choice for the whole run, exactly as if you had answered yes to the
# very first conflict prompt.
#YES_TO_ALL_CONFLICTS=skip

# Connection defaults (still overridable by --target-user / --target-port):
#TARGET_USER=root
#TARGET_PORT=22
